{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3432","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3432","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3432\",\"parent_revision\":\"012b38249739e292e0c3dceb569f74b650231712148d545a979f1cf2283335a1\",\"root_revision\":\"012b38249739e292e0c3dceb569f74b650231712148d545a979f1cf2283335a1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-06\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen `active-task set` receives an empty session id, it substitutes the sentinel \\\"_default\\\" and stores the record under that key. The write reports success.\\n\\nREPRODUCTION\\n\\ncli_active_task.py resolves `session_id or DEFAULT_SESSION_ID` on the set, get, update and clear paths, with DEFAULT_SESSION_ID = \\\"_default\\\".\\n\\nThe live context directory contains active-task-_default.json holding a real registration (plan P8c3a5756, task T6, parent issue 3182), and a sibling literally named active-task-${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID}.json holding another real registration (plan Pdea64bb9, task T1, same parent issue). Both dated 2026-09-04. Neither is test data.\\n\\nIMPACT\\n\\nAn unusable key is converted into a usable-looking one that every caller in the same state shares. \\\"I do not know which session I am\\\" becomes \\\"I am everyone\\\", so concurrent writers overwrite each other under one sentinel.\\n\\nThe read side compounds it: the hook resolves a session id from the transcript and looks that key up, so it never consults _default. A registration written there is invisible to the hook meant to consume it, and the failure is silent on both sides.\\n\\nThe literal-variable filename shows a second route to the same outcome: a shell that did not expand the variable produced a key that is neither valid nor rejected.\\n\\nUser-provided context: the argument for failing loudly is that a correlation record whose key cannot identify its owner has no correct use. Related to the session-id uniqueness defect filed alongside this. If that item rekeys the record away from session id, this normalisation may disappear rather than need a fix, so groom the two together.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session audit of cli_active_task.py, 2026-09-06\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"46919e194578fc01d25341d38f7cbbbee59b8ff1e44609b42a71bb7e03a1bf0f\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABTSBCdQ\"}"}